The Singleton Pattern was chosen particularly for BorrowingService because it is a class that we need access to for multiple different classes, all of which we aren't instantiating it any differently in. 

Using Singleton here allows for only one instance of BorrowingService to be used throughout the system, saving us from writing redundant code and instantiating a service object in multiple different methods. Our pattern is also synchronized, ensuring that only one thread executes the instance getter method at a time. This prevents the case where two instances are created by two threads at the same moment, and ensures we have exactly one instance.

One drawback from this particular design of Singleton is synchronization overhead, where the object may be created and afterwards pointless locking happens even though the instance is created. It's a slight performance cost. The Singleton Pattern itself can also introduce tight coupling, if classes constantly depend on it, then they will become tightly coupled to it, maintainability becomes difficult.

Patterns that could be alternatively used over Singleton could be Dependency Injection or a Factory Method. With Dependency Injection, it would have a similar impact as Singleton where you create a single instance and pass it to the classes that need it. Factory could decide which of potentially many instances could be handed to classes, whether its a new one or a instance that is shared. These patterns however would have little impact, specifically Dependency Injection, this is because it would be still doing the same behavior that Singleton is doing.